  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 311 AM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A ridge of high pressure is centered near\nthe International Border early this morning, providing us with \nideal conditions for frost in the Adirondack region and far \nnortheastern Vermont. The center of high pressure is expected to\nbecome positioned over the Gulf of Maine by this afternoon, \nleading to southerly channeled flow. Given dry and relatively \ncool air in place, Lake Champlain should once again have rather \nbreezy conditions beginning this afternoon with a long duration \nof south winds through tomorrow morning.\n\nAside from the local terrain effects and modest low level jet \ntonight, no significant weather is expected as a weakening cold \nfront approaches from the west tonight. Other than increasing clouds \nand milder conditions, there will be spotty light showers possible, \nwith a broad consensus on the timing being in the early Wednesday \nmorning hours and slightly greater chance of measurable rain in \nsouthern portions of our northern New York zones. Because the front \nlargely washes out over/just south of our region, a large area of \ncloudy skies will probably be present during the day, which will \npossibly limit heating on what otherwise would be the warmest day of \nthe week given fairly warm temperatures aloft. 925 millibar \ntemperatures especially over our southern areas will likely be above \nnormal (near the 75th percentile) during the afternoon, supporting \nhighs near 80 in the Upper Valley especially with mainly sunny skies \nor breaks in the clouds. With dewpoints increasing back into the low \n60s, it will be another short-lived bout of summer weather.\n\nThe next more substantial rainfall chance remains on track for \nThursday associated with weak overrunning, as high pressure remains \nto our north and a wave of low pressure passes to our south. The \nthermal gradient and dynamical support for rain looks modest at \nbest, so the footprint and intensity of rain should likewise be \nlimited. Trends in the data show better chances of showers generally \nin the afternoon/evening and across southern/central Vermont and \nsouthern portions of the Adirondacks. Rain cooled air will \ncomplicate the temperature forecast especially with an earlier onset \nof precipitation, but for many locations Thursday looks like the \nlast day for a while to see temperatures reach the 70s.\n\nA trough passing through Quebec will force a cold front through our \nregion Thursday night with little fanfare yet again, but bring in \nanother round of refreshing air on northwest flow. Nothing \nparticularly noteworthy with this air mass appears in the data at \nthis time with only modest advection; even in the global ensemble \ncluster with the lowest temperatures they only drop a bit below \nseasonal normals. As such, high temperatures ranging from the mid \n60s to low 70s amidst full sunshine look reasonable at this time. \nThe most cool and dry air appears to be timed for Friday night as \nhigh pressure builds in from the northwest.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Focus for the longer term portion of the forecast \ncontinues to be the low pressure system bringing rain to our region \nSaturday night into Sunday. Surface low will track from the Great \nLakes region northeastward just north of our area. There are still \nsome differences between models with this system, but general \nthought is for rain to spread into our region Saturday night, \ncontinuing into Sunday with most areas having a wetting rain. ECMWF \ndoes keep this system mostly south of our area, but GFS solution \nshows most areas having rain. WPC has placed our region in Marginal \nrisk for the excessive rain outlook.",
